Richard Llewellyn Smith <rls98@aber.ac.uk> writes:
> Recently my tourist had most of his gear nicked by a thieving nymph whilst
> helpless. Oddly, she left me my wand of digging (which is usually the
> first thing she takes), so I quickly stashed that under a boulder and went
> looking for a weapon. Finding nothing, I grabbed the first heavyish thing
> at hand (a statue of a newt IIRC) when she appeared and hit her over the
> head with it until she (eventually) relinquished my stuff.
> 
> So question: would I have been better off using my (unskilled) fists?

Yes, by my reading (though I may be mistaken). Non-weapons generally
do damage based on their weight, d(weight/100) where the size of the
die is clamped at 1 and 6; statues weigh 1.5 times the weight of the
monster they're of. So a newt statue weighs only 15 units and will do
d1 damage, while fists will do d2 damage for a non-martial-artist (and
there's no damage _penalty_ for being unskilled in bare-handed, and
50% of your hits would have exercised the skill, for what that's worth
to you).

